"High-end editorial blog cover and thumbnail (16:9 widescreen, 1K resolution, clean commercial graphic design, NOT photorealistic studio photography):\n\nBackground & Canvas: Warm, light, off-white/cream backdrop (#F5F7FA), evenly lit, minimal texture, soft and airy. Generous negative space. Never dark, never a studio table, never a photographic backdrop.\n\nTypography & Branding (Type-Led -- this is the dominant element of the entire composition, filling most of the left/center of the frame):\n1. Bold Headline: Large, bold, ultra-clean geometric sans-serif headline: \"Sediment Kills Heaters First\" filling the left ~60% of the frame, tight kerning, generous line spacing. Render it mostly in solid high-contrast near-black (#12242A), except render the single most important word of the headline in solid #FF6B4A for emphasis, exactly like a magazine pull-quote.\n2. Small Kicker Line: One short line of regular-weight text directly beneath the headline, in a muted gray, roughly a third the headline's size: \"A twenty-minute flush each fall keeps the hot water running through January and saves a…\"\n3. Small Top-Right Brand Wordmark: a small, sharp, high-contrast wordmark reading \"Acme Plumbing\" in solid #12242A with tight, clean letterforms. Keep it modest in size, never competing with the headline.\n\nSecondary Visual Accent (small, corner-positioned, NEVER the focal point -- a quiet supporting detail only, no larger than roughly 12% of the frame's width and height combined):\nA single thin line icon of a water heater with a drain valve.\nRender it as a single flat, thin two-color line icon with no shading, no gradients, no drop shadow, no 3D pedestal or perspective -- flat vector iconography, not a small illustration or product render.\n\nSTRICT TEXT RULE: The headline, the kicker line, and the wordmark are the ONLY text allowed anywhere in the image. Do NOT render any badges, tags, category pills, author name, byline, date, caption, watermark, or fine print. If you are not certain an element belongs to those, omit it entirely.\n\nAesthetic Style: The exact editorial standard of a modern SaaS/dev-tools blog (Stripe, Linear, Wall Street Journal digital covers): light, minimal, bold confident typography that carries the design, huge negative space, one small quiet supporting graphic. No dark studio tables, no flat-lay of physical objects, no holograms, no glowing effects, no stock-photo product-shot look, no clutter."
